# Seed Data and Reset Guide — {{project_slug}}

The app must not look empty in sandbox review. Every implemented feature must have representative demo data.

## Required Seeder Policy

- Each user-facing feature slice must add or update Laravel seeders.
- Create multiple focused seeders, not one opaque dump: demo users/roles, org/tenant structure, feature records, edge states, and relationship/linkage records.
- Seed at least 10 realistic records for each feature unless the feature naturally has fewer valid states.
- Seed all roles/personas needed to test the planned flows.
- Seed edge cases where useful: draft, active, approved, rejected, overdue, archived, failed, or escalated records.
- Seed enough demo users to test the whole app flow through the navbar User Switcher when `DEMO_MODE=true`.
- Keep demo credentials and reset commands documented here after every slice.

## Demo Mode

Sandbox `.env` defaults to:

```dotenv
DEMO_MODE=true
```

When `DEMO_MODE=true`:

- The app must be reviewable without logging in.
- The navbar must show a User Switcher populated from seeded demo users.
- Switching users must update the current demo identity and permissions so reviewers can test each role/persona flow.
- Seeders must include all users, roles, departments/tenants, and records needed for the switcher to exercise the full workflow.

When `DEMO_MODE=false`:

- The User Switcher must be hidden.
- Users must authenticate normally by email/username and password.
- Demo seed data may still exist in non-production environments, but access must use normal auth.

